{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["The reason for <b>and you shall love your neighbor as yourself</b> is an exaggeration, for the human heart cannot accept that one should love one's fellow as one loves oneself. Moreover, Rabbi Akiva has already come and taught: your life takes precedence over the life of your fellow (Bava Metzia 62a). Rather, it is a commandment of the Torah that one love one's fellow in every matter just as one loves oneself with respect to all good. And it is possible that because Scripture did not say 'and you shall love your neighbor' and equate them with the word 'your neighbor' — and likewise 'and you shall love him as yourself' (Leviticus 19:34), it is exact, so that its meaning is to equalize the love of both of them in one's mind. For sometimes a person loves one's fellow in known things — to benefit him with wealth but not with wisdom and the like — and if one loves in all things, one may still desire that one's beloved fellow attain wealth, property, honor, understanding, and wisdom, but not that he be equal to him; rather in one's heart one always desires to be greater than him in every good thing. And Scripture commands that this deficiency of jealousy not be in one's heart, but that one love the abundance of good for one's fellow just as a person does for oneself, and that one not set limits on the love. Therefore it is said of Jonathan (1 Samuel 20:17), 'for he loved him as his own soul,' because he removed the trait of jealousy from his heart and said (1 Samuel 23:17), 'and you shall reign over Israel,' and so on. And the matter of vengeance and bearing a grudge, our Rabbis have already explained it (Torat Kohanim, Kedoshim 4:10–11), that it concerns a matter involving no monetary obligation — 'lend me your sickle, lend me your axe' — for in a matter where one's fellow owes him money, such as damages and similar cases, he is not obligated to let it go, but he may demand it in court and collect from him, as derived from the verse 'as he did, so shall be done to him' (Leviticus 24:19), and he is himself obligated to pay just as one repays what one borrowed or what one stole. How much more so in a matter of life, that he may be vengeful and bear a grudge until he collects his brother's blood from his hand according to a court that instructs according to the laws of the Torah:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The Hebrew of the last clause (עַל פִּי בֵּית דִּין הַמּוֹרִים בְּמִשְׁפְּטֵי הַתּוֹרָה) is slightly elliptical; rendered hyper-literally as 'according to a court that instructs in the laws of the Torah.' The closing colon reproduces the colon found in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:56:12.213939343Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}